WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.235
Lesson 2.6.

00:00:02.235 --> 00:00:07.501
Advanced execution: limit, TWAP and intent-based orders.

00:00:07.501 --> 00:00:09.999
By the end, you'll use order types that give

00:00:09.999 --> 00:00:12.831
you better prices and protection than a plain swap.

00:00:14.131 --> 00:00:15.421
Here's the plan.

00:00:15.421 --> 00:00:17.259
On-chain limit orders, and the

00:00:17.259 --> 00:00:19.041
one real risk they carry.

00:00:19.041 --> 00:00:22.372
T.W.A.P. orders, and exactly why slicing

00:00:22.372 --> 00:00:24.866
a trade reduces its impact.

00:00:24.866 --> 00:00:28.231
Intent-based trading, and who actually fills your order.

00:00:28.231 --> 00:00:30.791
And a full worked example, comparing all

00:00:30.791 --> 00:00:34.101
three, on the exact same fifty-thousand-dollar trade.

00:00:35.401 --> 00:00:36.806
On-chain limit orders.

00:00:38.106 --> 00:00:40.446
Here's exactly how an on-chain limit

00:00:40.446 --> 00:00:42.391
order works, step by step.

00:00:42.391 --> 00:00:45.420
You sign an order off-chain, something like “sell

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:48.207
one E.T.H. at three thousand three hundred or

00:00:48.207 --> 00:00:51.479
better.” That order sits, waiting, usually costing no

00:00:51.479 --> 00:00:53.354
gas at all while it waits.

00:00:53.354 --> 00:00:54.631
It fills only once the

00:00:54.631 --> 00:00:56.874
market actually reaches your price.

00:00:56.874 --> 00:00:59.569
And if the market never gets there, it simply

00:00:59.569 --> 00:01:02.029
never fills, at no cost to you either way.

00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:05.558
Here's the one real risk worth naming

00:01:05.558 --> 00:01:07.729
plainly, since a limit order isn't a

00:01:07.729 --> 00:01:10.024
strictly better version of a swap.

00:01:10.024 --> 00:01:12.850
If the price never reaches your chosen level, your

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:15.343
order simply never fills, and you may miss a

00:01:15.343 --> 00:01:17.804
move you actually wanted to be part of.

00:01:17.804 --> 00:01:19.649
It's not a downside cost.

00:01:19.649 --> 00:01:21.927
It's an opportunity you didn't take, which

00:01:21.927 --> 00:01:23.199
is its own kind of cost.

00:01:24.499 --> 00:01:26.645
Worth being clear about when each one

00:01:26.645 --> 00:01:29.824
actually fits, since neither is universally better.

00:01:29.824 --> 00:01:32.714
A limit order fits when you're patient, and have

00:01:32.714 --> 00:01:35.664
a specific price in mind you'd genuinely be happy

00:01:35.664 --> 00:01:38.264
with, with no urgency to act right now.

00:01:38.264 --> 00:01:40.772
A plain swap fits when you need the trade

00:01:40.772 --> 00:01:43.998
to happen now, at whatever the current price actually

00:01:43.998 --> 00:01:46.984
is, urgency outweighing getting a specific number.

00:01:48.284 --> 00:01:49.374
Quick check.

00:01:49.374 --> 00:01:50.739
What's the actual risk of

00:01:50.739 --> 00:01:52.419
placing an on-chain limit order?

00:01:56.839 --> 00:01:59.398
The answer: it may simply never fill, if the

00:01:59.398 --> 00:02:02.525
market never reaches your chosen price, which can mean

00:02:02.525 --> 00:02:04.799
missing a move you wanted to be part of.

00:02:06.099 --> 00:02:07.039
TWAP orders.

00:02:08.339 --> 00:02:12.834
Here's exactly what a T.W.A.P. order does, mechanically.

00:02:12.834 --> 00:02:14.903
Take one large trade, and split

00:02:14.903 --> 00:02:17.465
it into many equal, smaller slices.

00:02:17.465 --> 00:02:19.972
Execute those slices spread out over a set

00:02:19.972 --> 00:02:22.138
period of time, not all at once.

00:02:22.138 --> 00:02:25.335
Each slice, being far smaller, sits much further back

00:02:25.335 --> 00:02:28.473
on the price-impact curve from Lesson two point two,

00:02:28.473 --> 00:02:31.378
so it moves the price far less individually.

00:02:31.378 --> 00:02:34.644
And the total impact, summed across every slice, comes

00:02:34.644 --> 00:02:37.138
out lower than one trade of the full size.

00:02:38.438 --> 00:02:41.032
Here's the trade-off a T.W.A.P. order

00:02:41.032 --> 00:02:44.063
actually carries, worth naming honestly.

00:02:44.063 --> 00:02:46.926
It takes real time to execute, by design,

00:02:46.926 --> 00:02:50.293
since that's exactly what reduces the impact.

00:02:50.293 --> 00:02:52.754
And during that window, the price can simply

00:02:52.754 --> 00:02:55.378
move against you, for reasons that have nothing

00:02:55.378 --> 00:02:57.483
to do with your own trade at all.

00:02:57.483 --> 00:02:59.578
Less impact from your own size.

00:02:59.578 --> 00:03:02.313
Real exposure to the market moving, meanwhile.

00:03:03.613 --> 00:03:06.496
Here's what actually setting one up looks like, in

00:03:06.496 --> 00:03:09.678
practice, on a wallet or aggregator that offers it.

00:03:09.678 --> 00:03:11.859
Choose your total trade size, and how

00:03:11.859 --> 00:03:13.823
many slices to split it into.

00:03:13.823 --> 00:03:16.572
Choose the time window it executes across,

00:03:16.572 --> 00:03:20.133
balancing less impact against more market exposure.

00:03:20.133 --> 00:03:23.791
And let it run; most implementations execute the remaining

00:03:23.791 --> 00:03:27.573
slices automatically, without a separate signature each time.

00:03:28.873 --> 00:03:29.963
Quick check.

00:03:29.963 --> 00:03:32.321
Why does splitting a large trade into

00:03:32.321 --> 00:03:34.928
a TWAP actually reduce total price impact?

00:03:39.348 --> 00:03:42.077
The answer: smaller slices sit further back

00:03:42.077 --> 00:03:44.495
on the price-impact curve, so each one

00:03:44.495 --> 00:03:47.409
moves the price less individually, even though

00:03:47.409 --> 00:03:50.138
the same total size eventually goes through.

00:03:51.438 --> 00:03:52.728
Intent-based trading.

00:03:54.028 --> 00:03:58.963
Here's the actual mechanism behind intent-based, or R.F.Q., trading.

00:03:58.963 --> 00:04:03.006
You state your intent plainly: “one hundred thousand U.S.D.C.

00:04:03.006 --> 00:04:06.788
into E.T.H., at least this much back.” Competing solvers,

00:04:06.788 --> 00:04:09.983
or market makers, see that intent and compete to

00:04:09.983 --> 00:04:13.635
fill it, sourcing liquidity from wherever they can find

00:04:13.635 --> 00:04:16.543
it, often across several venues at once.

00:04:16.543 --> 00:04:19.491
You get the best fill any solver was willing

00:04:19.491 --> 00:04:23.291
to offer, frequently with M.E.V. protection built in, and

00:04:23.291 --> 00:04:25.388
no gas paid on a failed attempt.

00:04:26.688 --> 00:04:30.733
Worth naming the one real trade-off intent-based trading carries,

00:04:30.733 --> 00:04:34.343
since “solvers compete” can sound like a free upgrade.

00:04:34.343 --> 00:04:37.627
Your actual fill quality depends entirely on how many

00:04:37.627 --> 00:04:41.277
solvers are actively competing for your specific order, and

00:04:41.277 --> 00:04:45.348
how well that particular protocol's solver system is designed.

00:04:45.348 --> 00:04:47.335
A thin solver market, or a poorly

00:04:47.335 --> 00:04:49.380
designed one, can leave you with a

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:51.308
fill no better than a plain swap.

00:04:52.608 --> 00:04:55.030
One real number, so intent-based trading

00:04:55.030 --> 00:04:57.516
doesn't feel like a niche experiment.

00:04:57.516 --> 00:05:02.511
Industry trackers have recorded intent-based and R.F.Q.-style trading

00:05:02.511 --> 00:05:05.936
growing to a meaningful share of total on-chain

00:05:05.936 --> 00:05:09.946
swap volume in recent years, across multiple chains.

00:05:09.946 --> 00:05:12.801
This is outside research, not this program's

00:05:12.801 --> 00:05:15.910
own data, but it's real, current infrastructure,

00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:18.701
worth actually comparing, not skipping past.

00:05:20.001 --> 00:05:21.091
Quick check.

00:05:21.091 --> 00:05:23.350
Who actually fills an intent-based order,

00:05:23.350 --> 00:05:24.856
and what do they compete on?

00:05:29.276 --> 00:05:32.453
The answer: competing solvers or market makers, who

00:05:32.453 --> 00:05:34.837
compete to offer you the best fill for

00:05:34.837 --> 00:05:38.075
what you stated you wanted, often sourcing liquidity

00:05:38.075 --> 00:05:39.786
from several venues at once.

00:05:41.086 --> 00:05:42.091
Worked example.

00:05:43.391 --> 00:05:47.036
Here's the baseline this entire comparison starts from.

00:05:47.036 --> 00:05:51.870
A single $50,000 trade, in this specific pool, carries

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:56.880
roughly 1.5% price impact, which is about $750, straight

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:00.396
out of the same formula from Lesson 2.2.

00:06:01.696 --> 00:06:04.586
Now the T.W.A.P. alternative, on the

00:06:04.586 --> 00:06:07.541
exact same fifty thousand dollars.

00:06:07.541 --> 00:06:09.153
Split it into ten slices,

00:06:09.153 --> 00:06:11.313
of five thousand dollars each.

00:06:11.313 --> 00:06:14.523
Execute those ten slices spread across one hour.

00:06:14.523 --> 00:06:16.486
Each slice, being a tenth of the

00:06:16.486 --> 00:06:19.343
size, carries far less impact individually.

00:06:19.343 --> 00:06:22.286
And the total impact across all ten slices comes

00:06:22.286 --> 00:06:25.228
out well below the seven hundred fifty dollars a

00:06:25.228 --> 00:06:27.930
single trade would have cost, as long as the

00:06:27.930 --> 00:06:30.873
price doesn't trend against you during that hour.

00:06:32.173 --> 00:06:34.158
And the third option, on that same

00:06:34.158 --> 00:06:37.803
fifty thousand dollars, works completely differently again.

00:06:37.803 --> 00:06:40.406
An intent-based order may beat both of the

00:06:40.406 --> 00:06:43.614
other two, by sourcing liquidity from several venues

00:06:43.614 --> 00:06:47.003
at once, through competing solvers, rather than working

00:06:47.003 --> 00:06:49.543
against a single pool's depth at all.

00:06:49.543 --> 00:06:52.075
The only way to actually know, on any

00:06:52.075 --> 00:06:54.875
given day, is to compare all three quotes

00:06:54.875 --> 00:06:57.607
side by side, not assume one always wins.

00:06:58.907 --> 00:07:01.206
Put all three side by side, for this

00:07:01.206 --> 00:07:05.607
exact fifty-thousand-dollar trade, and here's the actual decision.

00:07:05.607 --> 00:07:08.380
A single swap: instant, but the full seven

00:07:08.380 --> 00:07:11.582
hundred fifty dollars of impact, all at once.

00:07:11.582 --> 00:07:15.207
A T.W.A.P.: less impact, but real time exposure

00:07:15.207 --> 00:07:18.302
to the price moving during that hour.

00:07:18.302 --> 00:07:20.818
An intent order: potentially the best of

00:07:20.818 --> 00:07:22.721
all three, but only as good as

00:07:22.721 --> 00:07:25.912
that protocol's actual solver competition, that day.

00:07:27.212 --> 00:07:29.490
Here's how to actually get all three quotes

00:07:29.490 --> 00:07:32.017
yourself, before committing to any of them.

00:07:32.017 --> 00:07:34.052
Get a standard swap quote, direct or

00:07:34.052 --> 00:07:36.552
through an aggregator, as your baseline.

00:07:36.552 --> 00:07:40.419
Check whether that same venue or wallet offers a

00:07:40.419 --> 00:07:44.822
T.W.A.P. option, and get its estimated total impact.

00:07:44.822 --> 00:07:47.827
And check whether an intent-based venue is available

00:07:47.827 --> 00:07:49.981
for this pair, and get its quote too,

00:07:49.981 --> 00:07:52.192
before picking whichever nets the most.

00:07:53.492 --> 00:07:54.952
Here's your checklist.

00:07:54.952 --> 00:07:57.237
Do it now, on every large trade.

00:07:57.237 --> 00:08:00.078
Compare a plain swap, a T.W.A.P., and an

00:08:00.078 --> 00:08:03.842
intent-based quote, side by side, before choosing.

00:08:03.842 --> 00:08:05.877
Any limit order you place has an

00:08:05.877 --> 00:08:09.322
expiry you've actually chosen, not left indefinite.

00:08:09.322 --> 00:08:12.393
And you know, specifically, which order types you're

00:08:12.393 --> 00:08:15.232
using include M.E.V. protection, and which don't.

00:08:16.532 --> 00:08:18.868
One last honest point, before the recap,

00:08:18.868 --> 00:08:21.089
since these three tools can sound like

00:08:21.089 --> 00:08:24.067
they replace everything earlier in this module.

00:08:24.067 --> 00:08:27.830
They don't replace slippage discipline, checking price impact,

00:08:27.830 --> 00:08:30.997
or protected routing from Lesson two point five.

00:08:30.997 --> 00:08:33.216
They sit on top of those habits, giving

00:08:33.216 --> 00:08:35.380
you more ways to execute a trade well,

00:08:35.380 --> 00:08:37.433
not a reason to skip the checks that

00:08:37.433 --> 00:08:39.652
still apply to every single one of them.

00:08:40.952 --> 00:08:42.197
Let's recap.

00:08:42.197 --> 00:08:45.629
On-chain limit orders fill only at your price, usually

00:08:45.629 --> 00:08:48.987
gasless while waiting, but may simply never fill.

00:08:48.987 --> 00:08:52.621
T.W.A.P. orders slice a large trade over time,

00:08:52.621 --> 00:08:55.869
reducing impact per slice, at the cost of

00:08:55.869 --> 00:08:59.262
real time exposure to the market moving.

00:08:59.262 --> 00:09:03.618
Intent-based trading lets competing solvers fill your order,

00:09:03.618 --> 00:09:06.974
often with M.E.V. protection, but only as good

00:09:06.974 --> 00:09:10.487
as that protocol's actual solver competition.

00:09:10.487 --> 00:09:12.480
And for any trade of real size,

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:15.034
comparing all three is the actual habit,

00:09:15.034 --> 00:09:16.716
not picking one by default.

00:09:18.016 --> 00:09:20.162
Compare a plain swap, a TWAP, and an

00:09:20.162 --> 00:09:22.956
intent quote, before your next large trade.

00:09:22.956 --> 00:09:27.761
Next up, Lesson 2.7: Advanced AMM design and LVR.
